  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nISOLATED SHOWERS AND SEASONABLE TEMPS TO BEGIN THE WORK WEEK:\n\nBroad southwesterly flow will dominate the start of the work week, \nmaintaining moisture advection across the region. Expect a \ncontinuation of cloud cover along with isolated showers through \nTuesday. Modest warm air advection will nudge high temperatures \ncloser to seasonal normals despite the increased cloud cover. While \nlocalized breezy conditions could accompany collapsing showers, \norganized convection is not anticipated.\n\nWIDESPREAD RAIN AND FLOOD POTENTIAL LATE-WEEK: \n\nBy Wednesday, a strong low pressure system drops southward along the \nCalifornia coast, while an upper level jet noses into the Four \nCorners region. These two features will work in tandem to bring \nremnant moisture from what is now Hurricane Trudy into the region, \nbringing the return of widespread precipitation chances. Initial \nmodel solutions depict the most likely time period for widespread \nshowers to be Wednesday night through Thursday night. It is also \nimportant to note that a large majority of the precipitation from \nthis system will fall as rain, with the pool of colder temperatures \nstaying well north of the forecast area. As a result, snow levels \nwill remain high, generally at or above 12,000 feet. Given the \nprospect of high-elevation rain falling onto antecedent snowpack and \nsaturated soils from last week's precipitation, flash flooding and \nhydrologic impacts will be monitored closely as the system \nprogresses. The highest amounts of rainfall are currently showing up \nin the southern mountains where 1-3 inches of rainfall are \npossible. The San Juans look to take the brunt from this system \nso locally higher amounts are possible. In fact, some of the \nriver forecasts in that area are reaching flood stage. Ensemble \nguidance supports PWATs upwards of 250-300% of normal, providing\nanother strong signal to monitor for potential impacts. Keep in\nmind that the forecast remains fluid this far out, and key \ndetails may shift over the coming days. Stay tuned for updates. \n\nPrecipitation chances continue into Friday afternoon, though \ncoverage will gradually taper off. Meanwhile, the low pressure \nsystem will be absorbed into the main flow, driving a frontal \npassage through the area. This will bring cooler temperatures on \nFriday, dropping highs by 5 to 10 degrees compared to Thursday and \npotentially taking overnight temperatures back below freezing.",
